<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
        <handout margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="Week7Fri">
            <title>Friday Week 7 - The Law of Sines</title>
<page>
        <objectives>
          <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Derive the Law of Sines.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use the Law of Sines to determine dimensions of triangles when possible.
                </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </objectives>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    The Law of Sines is the second of two tools we use when working with non-right triangles (the first being the Law of Cosines). The Law of Sines says that in a given triangle the ratio of the sine of an angle to the side opposite that angle is constant.
                </p>
            </introduction>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Deriving the Law of Sines</title>
<p>
    Consider the labeled triangle below (the same one from the Law of Cosines):
</p>
<image>
<prefigure label = "law-of-sines" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="(300,5,300,5)">
  <coordinates bbox="(-2,-2,9,5.5)">
    <definition>points=((8,0),(0,0),(6,5))</definition>
    <triangle vertices="points"
              />
    <angle-marker points="points" radius="30">
      <m>C</m>
    </angle-marker>
    <angle-marker points="((0,0),(6,5),(8,0))" radius="30">
      <m>B</m>
    </angle-marker>
    <angle-marker points="((6,5),(8,0),(0,0))" radius="30">
      <m>A</m>
    </angle-marker>
    <label anchor="(2.5,3)" alignment="east">
      <m>a</m>
    </label>
    <label anchor="(4,0)" alignment="south">
      <m>b</m>
    </label>
    <label anchor="(7,2.5)" alignment="east">
      <m>c</m>
    </label>
    <label anchor="(6,2.5)" alignment="west">
      <m>h</m>
    </label>
     <line p1="(6,5)" p2="(6,0)" dash="5 5 5"/>
    <angle-marker points="((6,5),(6,0),(0,0))" radius="20">
    </angle-marker>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<p workspace="3in">
    <em>Observation:</em> We can write <m>h</m> as <m>h = a\sin(C)</m> and as <m>h=c\sin(A)</m>. What does that get us?
</p>

<p workspace="1in">
    Thus, we have <term>The Law of Sines:</term>
</p>

</paragraphs>

</page>
<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Standard Use of the Law of Sines</title>

        <p>
           The Law of Sines is useful when you know one angle, the side length opposite that angle, and one additional measurement.
        </p>
        <sidebyside>
            <image>
<prefigure label = "non-right3" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="(100,5,100,5)">
  <coordinates bbox="(-2,-2,9,5.5)">
    <definition>points=((5,0),(-2,0),(9,4))</definition>
    <triangle vertices="points"
              />
    <angle-marker points="points" radius="30">
      <m>?</m>
    </angle-marker>
    <angle-marker points="((-2,0),(9,4),(5,0))" radius="50">
      <m>20^{\circ}</m>
    </angle-marker>
    <angle-marker points="((9,4),(5,0),(-2,0))" radius="15">
      <m>?</m>
    </angle-marker>
    <label anchor="(2.5,0)" alignment="south">
      <m>6</m>
    </label>
    <label anchor="(7,1.8)" alignment="east">
      <m>5</m>
    </label>
    <label anchor="(3,2.2)" alignment="east">
      <m>?</m>
    </label>

  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<p workspace="3in">
    Find the unknown dimensions of this triangle.
</p>
        
        </sidebyside>





</paragraphs>   



</page>

<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>The Ambiguous Case</title>
 <p>
          Use the Law of Sines to determine the angle in the upper right of the given triangle.
        </p>
        <sidebyside>
            <image>
<prefigure label = "non-right4" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="(100,5,100,5)">
  <coordinates bbox="(-2,-2,9,5.5)">
    <definition>points=((5,0),(-2,0),(9,4))</definition>
    <triangle vertices="points"
              />
    <angle-marker points="points" radius="30">
      <m>25^{\circ}</m>
    </angle-marker>
    <angle-marker points="((9,4),(5,0),(-2,0))" radius="20">
      <m>?</m>
    </angle-marker>
    <label anchor="(7,1.8)" alignment="east">
      <m>7</m>
    </label>
    <label anchor="(2.5,2.2)" alignment="east">
      <m>10</m>
    </label>

  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
<p workspace="3in">
</p>
        
        </sidebyside>
<p workspace="3in">
    How can you tell when there are multiple possibilities? (There was only one possibility for each angle in the last problem.)
</p>




</paragraphs>   


</page>

        

</handout>
